          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading in someone who has not been given a diagnosis of hypertension is classified as a finding, not a disease. In the US coding system, it sits in Chapter 18, \"Symptoms, signs and abnormal findings\" (R00–R99), under the circulatory and respiratory block (R00–R09). The coding summary notes that an \"elevated blood-pressure reading in someone with no diagnosis of hypertension\" is one of the codes in that block. (Source: ICD-10-CM Chapter 18 summary; ICD-10-CM Chapter 9 and I10 coding summaries.)\n\nThe diagnosis in the record, not the number itself, decides whether a case moves to the hypertension code (I10) in Chapter 9. The same guidance explains that a single high reading is not itself a diagnosis. Hypertension is based on repeated measurements over time. (Source: Hypertension condition summary; I10 coding summary.)\n\nThe guidance also notes that a reading can be pushed up by poor technique, such as an undersized cuff or an unsupported arm, so the way the reading was taken matters. (Source: Hypertension diagnostic thresholds guideline.)\n\nThis answer covers how the classification works, not what a particular reading means for a particular person. If a reading has been raised, the right next step is to talk with a clinician about repeat measurements and what they mean.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
